1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,503
Good morning.

2
00:00:00,503 --> 00:00:05,018
Today I'd like to present the findings of our Year 2 project on wildlife

3
00:00:05,018 --> 00:00:06,900
found in gardens throughout our city.

4
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:11,505
I'll start by saying something about the background to the project, then talk a

5
00:00:11,505 --> 00:00:13,479
little bit about our research techniques,

6
00:00:13,479 --> 00:00:16,110
and then indicate some of our interim findings.

7
00:00:16,110 --> 00:00:19,200
First of all, how did we choose our topic?

8
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:23,761
Well, there are four of us in the group and one day while we

9
00:00:23,761 --> 00:00:28,323
were discussing a possible focus, two of the group mentioned that they had seen

10
00:00:28,323 --> 00:00:29,300
yet more sparrow-hawks

11
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:33,433
-- one of Britain's most interesting birds of prey -- in their own city centre gardens

12
00:00:33,433 --> 00:00:37,270
and wondered why they were turning up in these gardens in great numbers.

13
00:00:37,270 --> 00:00:41,787
We were all very engaged by the idea of why wild animals would choose

14
00:00:41,787 --> 00:00:43,400
to inhabit a city garden.

15
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:48,804
Why is it so popular with wildlife when the countryside itself is becoming less

16
00:00:48,804 --> 00:00:49,190
so?

17
00:00:49,190 --> 00:00:54,132
The first thing we did was to establish what proportion of the urban land

18
00:00:54,132 --> 00:00:56,250
is taken up by private gardens.

19
00:00:56,250 --> 00:00:59,420
We estimated that it was about one fifth,

20
00:00:59,420 --> 00:01:04,969
and this was endorsed by looking at large-scale usage maps in the town land

21
00:01:04,969 --> 00:01:06,950
survey office-24% to be precise.

22
00:01:06,950 --> 00:01:12,600
Our own informal discussions with neighbours and friends led us to believe that many

23
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:17,040
garden owners had interesting experiences to relate regarding wild animal sightings,

24
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:21,970
so we decided to survey garden owners from different areas of the city.

25
00:01:21,970 --> 00:01:27,434
Just over 100 of them completed a survey once every two weeks for twelve

26
00:01:27,434 --> 00:01:32,899
months-ticking off species they had seen from a pro forma list-and adding the names

27
00:01:32,899 --> 00:01:34,460
of any rarer ones.

28
00:01:34,460 --> 00:01:39,200
Meanwhile, we were doing our own observations in selected gardens throughout the city.

29
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:43,488
We deliberately chose smaller ones because they were by far the most typical in

30
00:01:43,488 --> 00:01:44,100
the city.

31
00:01:44,100 --> 00:01:48,141
The whole point of the project was to look at the norm not the

32
00:01:48,141 --> 00:01:48,430
exception.

33
00:01:48,430 --> 00:01:53,563
Alongside this primary research on urban gardens, we were studying a lot of books

34
00:01:53,563 --> 00:01:58,697
about the decline of wild animals in the countryside and thinking of possible causes

35
00:01:58,697 --> 00:01:59,430
for this.

36
00:01:59,430 --> 00:02:00,927
So what did we find?

37
00:02:00,927 --> 00:02:01,227
Well,

38
00:02:01,227 --> 00:02:05,120
so much that I just won't have time to tell you about here.

39
00:02:05,120 --> 00:02:11,182
If you're interested in reading our more comprehensive findings, we've produced detailed graphic representations on the college web-site

40
00:02:11,182 --> 00:02:15,896
and of course any of the group would be happy to talk to you

41
00:02:15,896 --> 00:02:16,570
about them.

42
00:02:16,570 --> 00:02:17,770
Just email us.

43
00:02:17,770 --> 00:02:23,370
What we've decided to present today is information about just three species-because we felt

44
00:02:23,370 --> 00:02:28,170
these gave a good indication of the processes at work in rural

45
00:02:28,170 --> 00:02:30,570
and urban settings as a whole.

46
00:02:30,570 --> 00:02:38,150
The first species to generate a lot of interesting information was frogs.

47
00:02:38,150 --> 00:02:43,040
And there was a clear pattern here -- they proliferate where there is suitable water.

48
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:48,772
Garden ponds are on the increase, rural ponds are disappearing, leading to massive migration

49
00:02:48,772 --> 00:02:50,000
to the towns.

50
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:54,515
Hedgehogs are also finding it easier to live in urban areas -- this time

51
00:02:54,515 --> 00:02:59,377
because their predators are not finding it quite so attractive to leave their rural

52
00:02:59,377 --> 00:02:59,724
environment,

53
00:02:59,724 --> 00:03:02,850
so hedgehogs have a better survival rate in cities.

54
00:03:02,850 --> 00:03:04,469
We has lots of sightings,

55
00:03:04,469 --> 00:03:09,002
so all in all we had no difficulties with our efforts to count their

56
00:03:09,002 --> 00:03:09,650
numbers precisely.

57
00:03:09,650 --> 00:03:15,240
Our final species is the finest of bird singers, the song thrush.

58
00:03:15,240 --> 00:03:20,776
On the decline in the countryside, they are experiencing a resurgence in urban gardens

59
00:03:20,776 --> 00:03:24,730
because these days gardeners are buying lots of different plants

60
00:03:24,730 --> 00:03:27,412
which means there's an extensive range of seeds around,

61
00:03:27,412 --> 00:03:29,200
which is what they feed on.

62
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:34,502
Another factor is the provision of nesting places-which is actually better in gardens than

63
00:03:34,502 --> 00:03:35,259
the countryside.

64
00:03:35,259 --> 00:03:36,774
Hard to believe it,

65
00:03:36,774 --> 00:03:37,910
but it's true.

66
00:03:37,910 --> 00:03:42,216
Incidentally, we discovered that a massive new survey on song thrushes is about to

67
00:03:42,216 --> 00:03:42,832
be launched,

68
00:03:42,832 --> 00:03:45,600
so you should keep an eye open for that.

69
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:48,530
Now, I'd be happy to answer any questions you may have…
